From: Sergio Prado <sergio.prado@e-labworks.com>
Date: Mon, 15 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] plat-imx: tzc380: secure the TZASC background region 0

On i.MX 8M the DDR controller ignores the upper address bits, so the
secure DRAM is also reachable through address aliases (e.g. 0x1be000000
aliases 0xbe000000). Those aliases are not covered by the TZASC regions
configured by OP-TEE (DRAM, TZDRAM, shared memory) and therefore fall
through to the background region 0.

Earlier boot stages leave region 0 wide open to the normal world:
U-Boot SPL (enable_tzc380() writes 0xf0000000 to REGION_ATTRIBUTES_0)
and TF-A BL31 (bl31_tzc380_setup() programs region 0 with TZC_ATTR_SP_ALL).
OP-TEE only configures regions 1-3 and never touches region 0, so the
permissive setting survives. A compromised normal world can then read
secure memory through the aliases, bypassing TrustZone isolation.

OP-TEE is the last boot stage to program the TZASC and the only one that
locks it down (tzc_regions_lockdown()), and neither U-Boot nor TF-A lock
the per-region registers. Lock region 0 down to secure-only access right
before the lockdown to close the bypass regardless of what the earlier
stages left behind.

This backports the behaviour of upstream CFG_TZASC_REGION0_SECURE
(OP-TEE >= 4.10.0) into the NXP downstream OP-TEE used on i.MX 8M.

Reference: https://sigma-star.at/blog/2026/06/trustzone-intermezzo/

Upstream-Status: Backport [https://github.com/OP-TEE/optee_os/commit/443c5817de47f1bd19091b419806898070382a67]
Signed-off-by: Sergio Prado <sergio.prado@e-labworks.com>
---
diff --git a/core/arch/arm/plat-imx/conf.mk b/core/arch/arm/plat-imx/conf.mk
index d1fc2882e598..ff4e143e3182 100644
--- a/core/arch/arm/plat-imx/conf.mk
+++ b/core/arch/arm/plat-imx/conf.mk
@@ -617,6 +617,10 @@ CFG_PKCS11_TA ?= y
 CFG_CORE_HUK_SUBKEY_COMPAT_USE_OTP_DIE_ID ?= y
 CFG_TZC380 ?= y
 
+# Lock down the TZASC background region 0 to secure-only access (backport of
+# upstream CFG_TZASC_REGION0_SECURE, OP-TEE >= 4.10.0). See tzc380.c.
+CFG_TZASC_REGION0_SECURE ?= y
+
 # Almost all platforms include CAAM HW Modules, except the
 # ones forced to be disabled
 CFG_NXP_CAAM ?= y
diff --git a/core/arch/arm/plat-imx/tzc380.c b/core/arch/arm/plat-imx/tzc380.c
index ecc545be6948..178d57d9ab00 100644
--- a/core/arch/arm/plat-imx/tzc380.c
+++ b/core/arch/arm/plat-imx/tzc380.c
@@ -75,6 +75,18 @@ static TEE_Result imx_configure_tzasc(void)
 		region = imx_tzc_auto_configure(CFG_SHMEM_START, CFG_SHMEM_SIZE,
 						TZC_ATTR_SP_ALL, region);
 
+		/*
+		 * The i.MX DDR controller ignores the upper address bits, so
+		 * secure DRAM is also reachable through address aliases that are
+		 * not covered by the regions configured above and therefore fall
+		 * through to the background region 0. If earlier boot stages
+		 * (U-Boot SPL, TF-A) left region 0 open to the normal world, the
+		 * normal world can read secure memory via those aliases. Lock
+		 * region 0 down to secure-only access here to close that bypass.
+		 */
+		if (IS_ENABLED(CFG_TZASC_REGION0_SECURE))
+			tzc_configure_region(0, 0, TZC_ATTR_SP_S_RW);
+
 		if (tzc_regions_lockdown() != TEE_SUCCESS)
 			panic("Region lockdown failed!");
 
